/*
  core/masks/policy_behavior.spthy -- f_H for the SET_POLICY action class, CEREMONY-AGNOSTIC (AGNOSTIC_MASKS.md).
  PATHWAY B: a TASK-MEDIATED mistake with NO mask change. Unlike every other behaviour file, this one
  does NOT read !OutcomePolicy -- the outcome is determined by the TASK, not by the human's mask. The
  SAME Attentive user sets a SAFE policy under a clear control and an UNSAFE one (a Mistake) under a
  misleading control (the AWS S3 "Any Authenticated Users" case; sigma4 MisleadingTerminology).

  The control's design quality is an INTERFACE-level fact, not step data: this file reads the
  persistent !ControlDesign('set-policy', quality) row seeded by a named interface include
  (core/interface_clear_policy.spthy / core/interface_misleading_policy.spthy), exactly the way the
  detectors read !Demands rows. The 'set-policy' !Step carries NO !StepData -- payloads hold values
  or observables only; design judgments live in named seeds (repo MEMORY.md lesson 14). This makes
  the design quality a PROFILE assumption: include the clear seed and the unsafe policy is
  unreachable (P7); include the misleading seed and it is reachable (P5/S0/S1); include both to
  quantify over both designs. Each pid is answered once (answered_once), so with both seeds a trace
  commits each control to one design.

  RespMask(P,'Attentive') is gated by mask_state.spthy: the user is genuinely un-degraded, yet errs.
*/

rule Policy_clear [color=#27ae60]:
    [ !Step(P, pid, 'set-policy'), !ControlDesign('set-policy', 'clear') ]
  --[ SetPolicy(P, pid, 'safe'), RespMask(P,'Attentive'), Answered(pid) ]->
    [ PolicyDone(P, pid) ]

rule Policy_misleading [color=#27ae60]:
    [ !Step(P, pid, 'set-policy'), !ControlDesign('set-policy', 'misleading') ]
  --[ SetPolicy(P, pid, 'unsafe'), Mistake(P, pid), RespMask(P,'Attentive'), Answered(pid) ]->
    [ PolicyDone(P, pid) ]
